    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The sovereignist argument rests on the theory that mandatory uniformity violates national sovereignty and ignores the principle of comparative advantage, which dictates that nations should specialize in what they do best rather than forcing identical regulations. They argue that imposing high environmental and labor standards on developing economies creates an unfair competitive disadvantage for their industries, effectively punishing them for their level of development while protecting domestic incumbents from global competition. Furthermore, they contend that such rigid treaties lock out innovation by preventing countries from tailoring regulations to their specific economic stages, leading to stagnation rather than growth. Ultimately, the sovereignist view is that true global cooperation requires flexibility and mutual benefit, not a one-size-fits-all mandate that treats all nations as if they are economically identical.",
